  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1134 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nOn Monday, an upper shortwave trough will move northeast into Texas \nand bring a possibility of isolated to scattered showers to western \nparts of our area (primarily west of a Rotan to Robert Lee to Ozona \nline). Will have to monitor for the potential of rain chances to \nextend farther east with the arrival of the shortwave. Isolated to \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms will be possible on \nTuesday, mainly east of a Haskell to San Angelo to Sonora line when \nanother impulse aloft may affect the area. With fairly moist south-\nsoutheast low-level flow, expect considerable morning cloud cover \nwith skies partly to mostly cloudy in the afternoon Monday and \nTuesday. Temperatures will be seasonably warm with highs in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s and lows mostly in the upper 50s to lower \n60s. Expecting Wednesday to be dry and warm with highs 80-85 \ndegrees. \n\nRain chances will return Thursday into Saturday, when our area will \nhave southwest flow aloft and a few disturbances moving through. \nThis will be out ahead of a large scale upper trough which is \nprogged to evolve over the West Coast. Our area may have more \nappreciable chances for showers and a few thunderstorms during this \ntime when precipitable water values are progged to be 1.3 to 1.5 \ninches. Mostly cloudy skies should prevail with perhaps some \nintervals of sunshine. Daily highs are generally expected to be in \nthe mid 70s to lower 80s, with overnight lows mild and above normal \nin the upper 50s to lower 60s.",
